\section{Layer Definition}

The MazdoorLink platform is structured around a three-layer client-side
architecture, where each layer is responsible for a distinct set of
functions. Because the system uses a serverless approach, there is no
dedicated application server; all layers reside within the Flutter
application or are provided as managed cloud services consumed directly
by the client.

\begin{table}[H]
    \centering
    \caption{Layers Definition}
    \label{tab:layers}
    \vspace{0.2cm}
    \begin{tabular}{|p{4cm}|p{9cm}|}
        \hline
        \textbf{Layer} & \textbf{Description} \\
        \hline
        Presentation Layer & Handles all user interactions and renders the visual interface. It delivers distinct, role-based views tailored to different user types, supports localization with proper text-direction handling (both left-to-right and right-to-left layouts), and coordinates application navigation through a centralized routing system. \\
        \hline
        Business Logic Layer & Acts as the coordinator for the application, housing all client-side business rules and defining abstractions for external services. This layer encapsulates data persistence, remote API operations, and asset-handling mechanisms (such as image compression and storage) behind clean interfaces. It also accommodates placeholder integrations for planned future services and manages global application state to ensure data flows predictably throughout the system. \\
        \hline
        DataBase Layer & Houses the external storage, database, and identity management systems consumed by the client application. This layer manages secure user authentication (including session tokens and multi-factor validation), maintains real-time NoSQL data storage for active application states, and handles cloud-based media hosting for user profiles and identity verification documents. \\
        \hline
    \end{tabular}
\end{table}

\subsection{Presentation Layer}

The Presentation Layer occupies the top level of the MazdoorLink
architecture and is built entirely using the Flutter framework, targeting
both Android and iOS platforms from a single Dart codebase. This layer
renders three distinct role-specific interfaces housed in
\texttt{lib/screens/}: the Customer App, the Worker App, and the Admin
Dashboard. Navigation between screens is handled by a centralized route
generator in \texttt{mazdoor\_flow.dart} (\texttt{AppRoutes}), which
manages screen transitions and passes typed data arguments such as
\texttt{RecommendationArguments} during the hiring flow. A custom
bilingual engine (\texttt{AppLocalizations} and \texttt{bilingual()}
helpers) dynamically switches the entire UI between English and Urdu,
with correct right-to-left text rendering for Urdu. The Worker UI is
specifically designed for low-literacy users, featuring large one-tap
buttons, high-contrast colors, and Urdu as the default language.

\subsection{Service Integration Layer}

The Service Integration Layer sits between the Presentation Layer and the
Data Layer and contains all client-side business logic and service
abstractions. \texttt{workers\_service.dart} is the main repository class
that abstracts all Firestore operations including fetching workers, creating
job documents, and updating statuses, following the Repository Pattern.
\texttt{cloudinary\_service.dart} handles compressing local image files and
uploading them directly to the Cloudinary API, storing the returned public
URL in Firestore. Global application state — including the current language
preference and theme — is managed by an \texttt{AppScope} InheritedWidget
that sits at the root of the widget tree. Transient UI state such as form
inputs, toggle positions, and loading indicators is managed by standard
\texttt{StatefulWidget} instances. Stub service classes
(\texttt{speech\_service.dart} for Urdu voice navigation and
\texttt{translation\_service.dart} for automatic chat translation) are
defined in this layer and are targeted for full backend integration in
Phase~2.

\subsection{Data Layer}

The Data Layer contains all persistent storage and identity management
infrastructure used by the MazdoorLink platform. It is accessed directly
by the Flutter client through official SDKs without any intermediate
server. Firebase Authentication manages user identities, issues JWT session
tokens, and provides the unique \texttt{uid} used to link every user to
their data in Firestore. Cloud Firestore is the primary NoSQL real-time
database and the core data engine of the system; the Flutter app connects
directly to Firestore using secure snapshot streams to receive real-time UI
updates. The primary Firestore collections are \texttt{users},
\texttt{jobs}, \texttt{conversations}, \texttt{messages},
\texttt{complaints}, and \texttt{damage\_claims}. Cloudinary serves as the
media storage provider; when a worker uploads a CNIC or profile picture,
the image is sent directly from the device to Cloudinary, which returns a
public HTTPS URL that is then saved in the worker's Firestore document.

\section{Software Architecture}

Software architecture is described as the organization or structure of a
system, where the system represents a collection of components that
accomplish a specific function or set of functions. The architecture of the
MazdoorLink platform has been designed with careful consideration of five
major architectural decisions: the application type, the deployment
strategy, the appropriate technologies, the quality attributes, and the
crosscutting concerns.

\textbf{Application Type:} MazdoorLink is a cross-platform mobile
application developed using the Flutter framework, targeting both Android
and iOS operating systems from a single codebase. The application follows
a serverless client-side architecture where the Flutter frontend
communicates directly with Firebase services and the Cloudinary and Google
Maps APIs through their respective SDKs and REST interfaces, without a
custom application server.

\textbf{Deployment Strategy:} The mobile application is distributed through
the Google Play Store and Apple App Store. All backend data infrastructure
is provided by Google Firebase's managed, globally distributed cloud
platform, which eliminates the need for on-premises infrastructure and
enables automatic scaling. Media assets are stored on Cloudinary's managed
CDN. There are no custom servers or containerized services to deploy or
maintain.

\textbf{Appropriate Technologies:} The technology stack is composed of
Flutter and Dart for the mobile frontend; Firebase Authentication for
OTP-based user verification and JWT session management; Cloud Firestore as
the primary real-time NoSQL database; Cloudinary for compressed media
storage; the Google Maps Flutter Plugin and Geolocator for location
services; and the \texttt{image\_picker} plugin for device camera and
gallery access.

\textbf{Quality Attributes:} Scalability is achieved through Firebase's
automatic horizontal scaling and Cloudinary's CDN distribution.
Security is enforced through Firebase Security Rules, OTP-based
authentication, and encrypted data transmission over HTTPS. Performance
is ensured through Firestore's real-time snapshot streams, client-side
skill-intersection filtering, and image compression before Cloudinary
upload. Reliability is provided through Firebase's managed infrastructure
combined with job expiration timers that automatically invalidate
unanswered offers. Usability is addressed through role-specific interfaces
with bilingual support designed for users of different literacy levels.

\textbf{Crosscutting Concerns:} Security is applied uniformly across all
Firestore data access points through Firebase Security Rules that restrict
reads and writes to the correct authenticated role. Internationalization
is handled through Flutter's localization framework via the custom
\texttt{AppLocalizations} wrapper supporting English and Urdu. The worker
online/offline heartbeat mechanism addresses presence management by writing
\texttt{isOnline} and \texttt{lastSeenAt} to Firestore every 30 seconds
and automatically setting the worker offline when the app is backgrounded.

The architecture follows the \textbf{Serverless Client-Side} pattern,
organizing the system into three distinct layers as illustrated in
Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}. The Presentation Layer handles all user
interface rendering and interaction. The Service Integration Layer processes
client-side business rules and abstracts external service calls. The Data
Layer manages persistent storage and authentication through Firebase and
Cloudinary. This pattern was chosen because it eliminates server maintenance
overhead, provides built-in scalability, enables real-time data
synchronization through Firestore streams, and allows the team to focus
development effort entirely on the Flutter client.

