\section{Class Diagram}

The class diagram provides an overview of the MazdoorLink system by
describing the objects and classes inside the system and the relationships
between them. It models the domain-specific data structure and serves as
a blueprint for the Firestore document collections and the Flutter data
model classes used throughout the application.

The primary classes identified in the MazdoorLink system are as follows.
The \textbf{User} class serves as the base class from which the
\textbf{Customer} and \textbf{Worker} classes inherit, capturing shared
attributes such as \texttt{uid}, \texttt{phone}, \texttt{name},
\texttt{profileImageUrl}, \texttt{role}, and \texttt{status}. This
inheritance relationship reflects the \textbf{Generalization} design
pattern and maps directly to the single Firestore \texttt{users}
collection, where the \texttt{role} field distinguishes document types.
The \textbf{Admin} class is an independent actor with its own set of
responsibilities and a corresponding role value.

The \textbf{Worker} class extends \textbf{User} with additional attributes
including \texttt{serviceCategory}, \texttt{cnicImageUrl} (a Cloudinary
URL), \texttt{verificationStatus} (pending, approved, or rejected),
\texttt{isOnline}, \texttt{lastSeenAt}, and a \texttt{skills} array
containing nested skill objects each with a name and rate. The
\texttt{skills} array drives the client-side skill-intersection filtering
used in the intelligent worker matching flow.

The \textbf{Job} class represents a job document in the Firestore
\texttt{jobs} collection and contains attributes including \texttt{jobId},
\texttt{customerId}, \texttt{workerId}, \texttt{serviceCategory},
\texttt{selectedIssues}, \texttt{price}, \texttt{paymentMethod},
\texttt{locationCoordinates}, \texttt{status} (pending, accepted, working,
completed, declined, cancelled, or expired), \texttt{createdAt}, and
\texttt{expiresAt}. The expiration timer is evaluated client-side to
automatically hide offers that have not been accepted within the defined
window.

The \textbf{Conversation} class represents a document in the Firestore
\texttt{conversations} collection and aggregates a \texttt{messages}
subcollection. The \textbf{Message} class represents an individual chat
document within that subcollection, with attributes for \texttt{senderId},
\texttt{content}, and \texttt{timestamp}. The \textbf{Complaint} class
maps to the \texttt{complaints} Firestore collection and contains
\texttt{complaintId}, \texttt{customerId}, \texttt{jobId},
\texttt{description}, \texttt{evidenceUrls}, and \texttt{status}. The
\textbf{DamageClaim} class maps to the \texttt{damage\_claims} collection
and contains additional fields for \texttt{adminDecision} and
\texttt{deductionAmount}.

The class diagram reflects \textbf{Association}, \textbf{Aggregation},
and \textbf{Composition} relationships between these classes. A Customer
aggregates multiple Jobs; a Conversation composes multiple Messages; a
Worker is associated with multiple Jobs and Complaints. The
\textbf{Repository Pattern} is applied through \texttt{workers\_service.dart},
which encapsulates all Firestore read and write operations and exposes
typed stream methods (\texttt{streamWorkerJobs},
\texttt{streamAllJobs}, \texttt{streamWorkerEarnings}) to the Presentation
Layer.

