\section{Entity Relationship Diagram}

An entity relationship diagram (ERD) is a conceptual representation of the
data stored in the MazdoorLink system. The ERD identifies the primary
entities of the system, their attributes, and the relationships that exist
between them. Although the MazdoorLink platform uses Cloud Firestore, a
NoSQL document database, the entity relationship model is presented here
to provide a technology-independent conceptual view of the data domain.
During physical design, this model is mapped into Firestore collections
and document structures as described in Section~3.5.

The primary entities identified in the MazdoorLink data model are
\textbf{Customer}, \textbf{Worker}, \textbf{Admin}, \textbf{Job},
\textbf{Conversation}, \textbf{Message}, \textbf{Complaint}, and
\textbf{DamageClaim}.

The \textbf{Customer} entity has a one-to-many relationship with
\textbf{Job}, as a single customer may post multiple job requests over
time. The \textbf{Worker} entity also has a one-to-many relationship with
\textbf{Job}, as a worker may be assigned to multiple jobs across their
activity history. A \textbf{Job} has a one-to-one relationship with a
\textbf{Conversation}, as each accepted job creates exactly one chat
channel between the customer and the worker. A \textbf{Conversation}
has a one-to-many relationship with \textbf{Message}, as multiple
messages are exchanged within a single conversation. The
\textbf{Customer} entity has a one-to-many relationship with
\textbf{Complaint}, as a customer may raise multiple complaints over
time. A \textbf{Complaint} has a zero-to-one relationship with
\textbf{DamageClaim}, as not every complaint results in a formal damage
claim. The \textbf{Worker} entity is associated with any
\textbf{DamageClaim} filed against them through the related job
reference. The \textbf{Admin} entity manages both \textbf{Worker}
verification (through the \texttt{status} field on the \texttt{users}
document) and \textbf{DamageClaim} resolution.

